       =20
        <div class=3D"text cmp-di-text pl-0 pr-0 pl-mob-2 pr-mob-2 aem-Grid=
Column--phone--12 cmp-di-text--line-height-large cmp-di-text-bullets">

   =20

<div class=3D"hide-no-results-error-msg">
    <img src=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/content/dam/insights/icons/svg/ico=
n-alert.svg"> =20
</div>
<div>
    <div class=3D"cmp-text ">
        <p><b>Priorities</b>. Chief information officers (CIOs) in Deloitte=
=E2=80=99s 2025 Tech Executive Survey singled out harnessing the full poten=
tial of AI, data, and analytics as the area where they=E2=80=99re spending =
most of their time and energy.<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/e=
n/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/ai-future-it-funct=
ion.html#endnote-5" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 5">5</a></sup>&=
nbsp;While AI has been top of mind for many executives in the past, generat=
ive and agentic AI have placed it at the top of the tech organization=E2=80=
=99s agenda=E2=80=94and they=E2=80=99re investing accordingly. The percenta=
ge of tech budgets allocated to AI is expected to rise significantly over t=
he next two years, from 8% to 13% on average, highlighting how AI is moving=
 from experimentation to core strategy.<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte=
.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/ai-future=
-it-function.html#endnote-6" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 6">6</=
a></sup></p>
<p><a href=3D"https://amedeloitte-my.sharepoint.com/personal/agwagh_deloitt=
e_com/Documents/Documents/Transfer/Work/TL/Activations/agwagh/US188550_Hann=
ah/US%20188550_TT2026_Business%20of%20Technology_FINAL%20FOR%20AEM%20AND%20=
DPF.docx#_edn2" name=3D"_ednref2"></a><b>People</b>. Nearly 70% of tech lea=
ders from the same survey plan to grow their teams in direct response to ge=
n AI<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technolo=
gy-management/tech-trends/2026/ai-future-it-function.html#endnote-7" aria-l=
abel=3D"Endnotes reference number 7">7</a></sup>=E2=80=94a clear shift from=
 fears of job loss to a strategy of augmentation and specialization. AI=E2=
=80=99s continued momentum is also creating new roles, like the chief peopl=
e and digital technology officer at Moderna and forward-deployed engineers,=
 as well as increasing the presence of others.<sup><a href=3D"https://www.d=
eloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/ai=
-future-it-function.html#endnote-8" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number=
 8">8</a></sup>&nbsp;For instance, the number of AI architect roles is expe=
cted to almost double, from 30% today to 58% in the next two years.<sup><a =
href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-managemen=
t/tech-trends/2026/ai-future-it-function.html#endnote-9" aria-label=3D"Endn=
otes reference number 9">9</a></sup>&nbsp;AI is driving new ways of working=
 and is no longer a =E2=80=9Cplug-and-play=E2=80=9D tool but a technology t=
hat requires thoughtful design, integration, and governance=E2=80=94tasks t=
hat demand specialized expertise.</p>
<p><b>Purpose</b>. As AI plays a key role in CEOs=E2=80=99 current and futu=
re strategies,<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topic=
s/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/ai-future-it-function.html#endnote=
-10" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 10">10</a></sup>&nbsp;the mand=
ate of the tech organization is changing. CEOs today look to tech leaders t=
o drive business strategy, not just run IT. Most large enterprises (66% in =
the Tech Exec Survey) view their tech org as a revenue generator rather tha=
n a service center, and when asked what the tech org=E2=80=99s role is in s=
haping the business, the top response from Deloitte=E2=80=99s Tech Spending=
 Outlook was =E2=80=9Cstrategic leader: enabling the overall business strat=
egy with a focus on technology.=E2=80=9D<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitt=
e.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/ai-futur=
e-it-function.html#endnote-11" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 11">=
11</a></sup>&nbsp;The increasing number of CIOs reporting directly to CEOs =
(65% in 2025<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/=
technology-management/tech-trends/2026/ai-future-it-function.html#endnote-1=
2" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 12">12</a></sup>&nbsp;versus 41%=
 in 2015<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/tech=
nology-management/tech-trends/2026/ai-future-it-function.html#endnote-13" a=
ria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 13">13</a></sup>) further signals th=
at technology and AI are not just operational concerns=E2=80=94they=E2=80=
=99re central to growth, innovation, and competitive positioning. The purpo=
se of the tech organization is expanding from =E2=80=9Ckeep the lights on=
=E2=80=9D to =E2=80=9Clight the way forward.=E2=80=9D</p>

    </div>
</div>

   =20

</div>
<div class=3D"text cmp-di-text pt-1 pb-1 pl-mob-2 pr-mob-2 aem-GridColumn--=
phone--12 cmp-di-text--line-height-large cmp-di-text-bullets">

   =20

<div class=3D"hide-no-results-error-msg">
    <img src=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/content/dam/insights/icons/svg/ico=
n-alert.svg"> =20
</div>
<div>
    <div id=3D"strategies-preparing-AI-powered-future" class=3D"cmp-text ">
        <h2>Strategies to prepare for an AI-powered future</h2>
<p>Organizations are actively assessing their tech operating models as AI g=
ains momentum. In fact, when asked how they=E2=80=99re evolving their tech =
op models to meet evolving business demands, just 1% of surveyed IT decisio=
n-makers said they had no major changes underway.<sup><a href=3D"https://ww=
w.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026=
/ai-future-it-function.html#endnote-14" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference nu=
mber 14">14</a></sup></p>
<p>The journey to preparing for an AI-driven future will vary depending on =
organizational maturity and priorities, among other factors, and will likel=
y start with increasing the adoption of AI and automation. Beyond that, her=
e=E2=80=99s how organizations are planning for an AI-driven future, today.<=
/p>
<h3>Modernization starts with business problems, not technology</h3>
<p>Seventy-one percent of surveyed organizations are currently modernizing =
core infrastructure to support AI implementation, and 23% are investing 6% =
to 10% of annual revenue in modernizing core enterprise systems.<sup><a hre=
f=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/t=
ech-trends/2026/ai-future-it-function.html#endnote-15" aria-label=3D"Endnot=
es reference number 15">15</a></sup>&nbsp;But recognition isn=E2=80=99t eno=
ugh; the key is solving real business problems, not just upgrading technolo=
gy.</p>
<p>=E2=80=9CModernization is not about technology for technology=E2=80=99s =
sake; it=E2=80=99s about addressing fundamental business problems like cost=
s, go-to-market issues, and so on,=E2=80=9D says Alan Davidson, CIO of Broa=
dcom. =E2=80=9CAI is a good example. The technology is evolving at such a r=
ate that conversations taking place about AI today are very different from =
those that happened six months ago, so it=E2=80=99s important to have a tac=
tical plan. Without focusing on a specific business problem and the value y=
ou want to derive, it could be easy to invest in AI and receive no return.=
=E2=80=9D<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/tec=
hnology-management/tech-trends/2026/ai-future-it-function.html#endnote-16" =
aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 16">16</a></sup></p>
<h3>Architectures designed for modularity and observability</h3>
<p>The AI-driven, future-ready enterprise can=E2=80=99t be built on legacy =
platforms patched together for survival, which may explain why 66% of surve=
yed organizations are piloting or exploring options around AI-enhanced ente=
rprise architecture.<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights=
/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/ai-future-it-function.html#e=
ndnote-17" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 17">17</a></sup>&nbsp;Wh=
en emerging technologies are used in conjunction with an end-to-end enterpr=
ise focus, they can deliver amplified value. New architectures can be desig=
ned for modularity and observability,<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.c=
om/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/ai-future-i=
t-function.html#endnote-18" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 18">18<=
/a></sup> which, at its core, is the ability to see, understand, and optimi=
ze a system by analyzing its external outputs.</p>
<p>=E2=80=9CAt Western Digital, we=E2=80=99re developing an observability a=
rchitecture to help us take a holistic approach to managing our tech landsc=
ape,=E2=80=9D says Sesh Tirumala, CIO of Western Digital. =E2=80=9CWe=E2=80=
=99re not waiting for perfect AI solutions; we=E2=80=99d rather fail fast o=
n small pilots than miss the wave entirely.=E2=80=9D<sup><a href=3D"https:/=
/www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2=
026/ai-future-it-function.html#endnote-19" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference=
 number 19">19</a></sup></p>
<p>The Coca-Cola Company is also prioritizing a modular approach. =E2=80=9C=
For global organizations, one size rarely fits all. Ways of working are not=
 the same everywhere throughout the world. Our approach has been to build a=
 modular architecture and a set of guiding core principles supported by an =
agile team able to operate at speed while localizing as needed,=E2=80=9D sa=
ys senior vice president and CIO Neeraj Tolmare.<sup><a href=3D"https://www=
.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/=
ai-future-it-function.html#endnote-20" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference num=
ber 20">20</a></sup>&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>
<h3>Human=E2=80=93machine collaboration at the heart of tech talent strateg=
y</h3>
<p>Recent Deloitte research highlights the rapid evolution of the tech tale=
nt landscape. As organizations adopt emerging technologies, the most antici=
pated new roles include:</p>
<ul><li>Human-AI collaboration designers, responsible for crafting seamless=
 interactions between people and intelligent systems</li><li>Edge AI and em=
bedded systems engineers, who bring AI capabilities directly to devices and=
 connected infrastructure</li><li>Data quality specialists for synthetic da=
ta, ensuring that the training data fueling AI systems is trustworthy and r=
epresentative</li><li>AI prompt engineers and model trainers, optimizing AI=
 outputs and tailoring models to specific business needs<sup><a href=3D"htt=
ps://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-tren=
ds/2026/ai-future-it-function.html#endnote-21" aria-label=3D"Endnotes refer=
ence number 21">21</a></sup></li></ul>
<p>To make AI work in practice, consider how it can be both an upskilling e=
ngine and a tool to bridge knowledge gaps. =E2=80=9CEven if you=E2=80=99re =
not a JavaScript expert or a product manager expert, AI can help bridge tha=
t gap, or even fill that gap,=E2=80=9D says Gene Kim, researcher and coauth=
or of <i>The Phoenix Project</i> and the newly released book, <i>Vibe Codin=
g</i>.<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/techno=
logy-management/tech-trends/2026/ai-future-it-function.html#endnote-22" ari=
a-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 22">22</a></sup>&nbsp;As organizations=
 reconsider their tech talent strategy, it=E2=80=99s helpful to think about=
 what degree of functional expertise they=E2=80=99ll need on their teams gi=
ven what AI can do, he adds. That can help illuminate where to focus any up=
skilling or reskilling efforts. (See <a></a><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte=
.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/ai-future=
-it-function.html#sidebar-1">sidebar for the full Q&amp;A</a>.)</p>
<p>While AI can democratize capabilities and expertise, tomorrow=E2=80=99s =
competitive edge will likely not simply come from adopting AI tools but fro=
m building teams that can design, manage, and evolve the way humans and mac=
hines work together. The future isn=E2=80=99t human <i>or</i> machine, but =
rather human <i>and</i> machine.<sup><a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us=
/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/ai-future-it-fun=
ction.html#endnote-23" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 23">23</a></=
sup></p>

