<div class=3D"cmp-di-aside__content" id=3D"sidebar-2">
    <h3 class=3D"cmp-di-aside__content__title">
        From writer to director: Steve Yegge on the software developer=E2=
=80=99s transformation</h3>
    <div class=3D"cmp-di-aside__content__text js-cmp-di-aside__content__tex=
t" id=3D"aside-content-sidebar-2">
       =20


<div class=3D"aem-Grid aem-Grid--12 aem-Grid--default--12 ">
   =20
    <div class=3D"text aem-GridColumn aem-GridColumn--default--12">

   =20

<div class=3D"hide-no-results-error-msg">
    <img src=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/content/dam/insights/icons/svg/ico=
n-alert.svg"> =20
</div>
<div>
    <div class=3D"cmp-text ">
        <p>A software engineer with more than 30 years of industry experien=
ce, Steve Yegge is the coauthor of the book <i>Vibe Coding </i>(with Gene K=
im; see the previous sidebar =E2=80=9C<a href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/u=
s/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends/2026/ai-future-it-fu=
nction.html#sidebar-1">Vibe shift: Gene Kim on AI-powered coding in enterpr=
ise IT</a>=E2=80=9D). Yegge has written over a million lines of production =
code in more than a dozen languages and has led multiple teams of up to 150=
 people each. He=E2=80=99s currently an engineer at Sourcegraph, working on=
 AI coding assistants.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><b>Q: How is AI coding affecting the tech function?</b></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><b>A:</b> IT is a layered activity. We=E2=80=99re losing the bottom laye=
r, code generation. Tasks or roles continuously get pushed down into hardwa=
re or software, and humans get pushed up the ladder. A lot of engineering a=
ctivity involves design, merging workstreams, and leading teams. Everyone=
=E2=80=99s getting pushed up in that direction because AI is writing code.<=
/p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>It also means nonprogrammers are entering the IT function. Roles like pr=
oduct managers and UX designers are helping with coding because we=E2=80=99=
re using AI to produce these shared artifacts. There=E2=80=99s a translatio=
n layer between the business and IT that we=E2=80=99ve never had before. We=
=E2=80=99re seeing small teams=E2=80=94maybe an engineer, a financial analy=
st, and a marketing person=E2=80=94create software.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><b>Q: How will this change the software engineer=E2=80=99s role?</b></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><b>A:</b> You can=E2=80=99t trust everything to AI. Eventually, a human =
needs to look at it. It=E2=80=99s like an old-school technical program mana=
ger who used to manage teams of engineers, but now you=E2=80=99re managing =
fleets of AI agents. But agents can=E2=80=99t solve everything. They can wo=
rk much faster than a human, but our ambitions will get so much bigger. All=
 the projects that we ever wanted to do, we=E2=80=99ll be able to do now, b=
ut it will take this constant course-correcting and babysitting and shepher=
ding AI.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>As the AI [tools] get smarter, more nonprogrammers will be able to do th=
is [oversight] over the next few years. But right now, it=E2=80=99s all abo=
ut programmers and their ability to be neuroplastic enough to adapt to this=
 entirely new way of working, where they=E2=80=99re essentially directing A=
I.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><b>Q: How do you measure developer productivity in this environment?</b>=
</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><b>A:</b> Companies have been trying to figure this out since AI-powered=
 code completions appeared back in 2022. With code completions, the AI woul=
d autocomplete the line of code you were writing, and you accepted or ignor=
ed it. The productivity metric was the acceptance rate.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>That measure of productivity vanished almost overnight when chat-based c=
oding tools came along, because [now] all you do is make a request in chat,=
 the AI writes the code, and you copy and paste it. It was harder to find g=
ood metrics because the improvements were more varied and context-dependent=
 than a simple acceptance rate.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Now we have coding agents, where the AI can use tools to run the code it=
self, see the results, and iterate without you having to manually copy-past=
e and relay information back and forth. People who use coding agents are 10=
 times more productive than people who don=E2=80=99t, by any measure that y=
ou pick: lines of code, commits, actual business outcomes. It=E2=80=99s so =
obviously an order of magnitude larger than the people who aren=E2=80=99t u=
sing the coding agents that companies don=E2=80=99t even try to measure it.=
 Then the discussion becomes what to do at performance review time when you=
=E2=80=99re trying to compare people who are 10 times more productive than =
their peers.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><b>Q: What are your thoughts on hiring developers in the AI era?</b></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><b>A:</b> It=E2=80=99s a tough time for new entrants into the field, but=
 my take is that people are being overcautious and they=E2=80=99re under-hi=
ring. They=E2=80=99re standing in the way of having an army of brilliant ju=
nior programmers building the next-generation thing that could launch the c=
ompany to the top of its category.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>People who are adaptable and neuroplastic have always been needed, but n=
ow they=E2=80=99re more important than ever. Hire people who don=E2=80=99t =
have a lot of baggage, not the ones who say, =E2=80=9CI won=E2=80=99t do X,=
 I won=E2=80=99t do Y.=E2=80=9D Invest in them, train them, and give them t=
he flexibility to make mistakes and learn from them as an organization. Com=
panies that do this are going to be super successful. <sup><a href=3D"https=
://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/topics/technology-management/tech-trends=
/2026/ai-future-it-function.html#endnote-34" aria-label=3D"Endnotes referen=
ce number 34" tabindex=3D"-1">34</a></sup></p>

    </div>
</div>

